{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The Executive baseline distinguishes frames from actors. Activation binds an actor to a frame. Executive, Worker and Reviewer are finite evaluation frames available for applicable engagements, not actor identities or an organisational hierarchy.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1523,
          "quote": "Frames and actors are separate identities: activation binds an actor to\na frame",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F17.%20Actor%20binding%20and%20independence",
          "start_line": 1515
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1811,
          "quote": "Executive, Worker and Reviewer are available finite evaluation frames,\n   activated only where applicable, not a hierarchy or actor identities;",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F22.%20Closure",
          "start_line": 1810
        }
      ],
      "key": "executive_baseline_frames",
      "label": "Executive baseline frames"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Profile qualification applies frame qualification and method self-qualification under EVALUATION §7, creating no separate protocol. A profile trial evaluates this profile and its declared composition with the exact REFERENCE_FRAME basis; neither becomes a peer authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1550,
          "quote": "Qualification of this profile applies frame qualification and method\nself-qualification (EVALUATION §7); it creates no other qualification\nprotocol",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F18.%20Profile%20qualification",
          "start_line": 1542
        }
      ],
      "key": "profile_qualification",
      "label": "Profile qualification and representative trial"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["A Product adopts the optional Executive baseline."],
      "definition": "The Executive Baseline Adoption Additions template supplements the §16 adoption record and the Project Reference-Frame Basis and starter template owned by PRODUCT_DEFINITION §7. It requests: exact profile identity; Executive, Worker and Reviewer declaration or acquisition references; role-specific capability, authority and independence bindings; Product severity and priority scales, current-boundary cutoff, non-waivable hard stops and Reviewer-to-Executive triage relation; referenced ACTIVATION §3 construction-availability mapping, with project refinements mapped to their parent value's core value; declarations, non-material evidence or residuals for all twelve generic families; Product-testing claims, frame topology, material combination populations, scenario matrix, oracles and required/forbidden authoritative paths; profile-specific activation-refusal, closed-result, conjunction and return relations; and qualification evidence and residuals.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1668,
          "quote": "# Executive Baseline Adoption Additions",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F19.%20Baseline-specific%20basis%20and%20activation%20additions%2FProject-basis%20additions",
          "start_line": 1651
        }
      ],
      "key": "baseline_adoption_additions",
      "label": "Executive Baseline Adoption Additions template"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The adoption record owned by §16 Adopt this baseline incorporates both its own list and the profile-specific additions as one record. Its statement decides any item appearing in both lists.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1642,
          "quote": "an adopting Product\nrecords both lists as one record, and where an item appears in both, the\n§16 statement decides it",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F19.%20Baseline-specific%20basis%20and%20activation%20additions%2FProject-basis%20additions",
          "start_line": 1640
        }
      ],
      "key": "baseline_adoption_record",
      "label": "Baseline adoption record"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Frame-specific additions extend the generic minimal activation binding: Worker execution intake and applicable mutation grant details; Reviewer claim, assessment, independence and technical-triage details; Executive Product, mandate, evaluation, policy, result and disposition details.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1681,
          "quote": "Every activation first carries the generic binding of REFERENCE_FRAME §11\n(minimal activation binding).",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F19.%20Baseline-specific%20basis%20and%20activation%20additions%2FActivation%20additions",
          "start_line": 1673
        }
      ],
      "key": "baseline_activation_additions",
      "label": "Baseline activation additions"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An observation-to-first-diagnosis table routes evaluation without predetermining a verdict.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1686,
          "quote": "The table routes evaluation; it does not predetermine a verdict",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F20.%20Failure%20classification",
          "start_line": 1685
        }
      ],
      "key": "failure_classification",
      "label": "Failure classification"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Thirty-two identified ways of misapplying the optional profile, covering frame identity, grants, result routing, review independence, evidence, testing, generic families and triage.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1721,
          "quote": "The profile is misapplied when",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F21.%20Anti-patterns",
          "start_line": 1721
        }
      ],
      "key": "profile_misapplication",
      "label": "Profile anti-patterns"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["An exact outcome and engagement branch have been selected."],
      "definition": "Baseline application to one outcome evaluates only applicable obligations. Work-item closure applies the existing TICKETS §7 condition, not Reviewer approval or the occurrence of a review. A closed indeterminate result does not satisfy an unresolved obligation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1803,
          "quote": "Only the obligations applicable to the exact selected outcome and\nengagement branch are evaluated.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F22.%20Closure",
          "start_line": 1792
        }
      ],
      "key": "baseline_closure",
      "label": "Branch-relative baseline closure"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The source-disposition table records routes from rc.2 STDO_REFERENCE_FRAME_BASELINE.md anchors to successor loci. Conserved rows are said to be restated in tenant text; routes are provenance only, including duplicate listings in ASSURANCE and EVALUATION. Heading-only predecessor rows are marked not applicable. Several synthetic child rows and the first failure-classification row describe proposed supersessions pending the Product owner's acceptance.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1885,
          "quote": "Every conserved row is restated in tenant text at its locus; a route is\nprovenance only.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EXECUTIVE_BASELINE.md#EXECUTIVE_BASELINE%2F23.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line": 1883
        }
      ],
      "key": "baseline_source_dispositions",
      "label": "Baseline predecessor-disposition ledger"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The subject's Tier 1 FOUNDATIONS member owns the vocabulary of standing; ontology of frames, authority, lineage and work boundaries; construction accounts and imports; P0–P6 commitments, consequence-trace format and family index; composition, conservation, core results, conjunction and categorical-demonstration requirements; and hypotheses. Other members import or refine these definitions without redefining their owner.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 13,
          "quote": "Tier 1. Owns the vocabulary of standing (§1);",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS",
          "start_line": 3
        }
      ],
      "key": "foundations_member",
      "label": "FOUNDATIONS member"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A foundational claim is a commitment or hypothesis and has exactly one standing: recovered, an adopted premise consolidating named source rules without treating them as proof; independent, an adopted premise argued by a case where other commitments hold and it fails; derived, a consequence with its premises named; or hypothesis, established by no method clause or argument, not law and retained for falsification. This differs from what a result establishes about its predicate and from a successor's disposition of a predecessor claim.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 40,
          "quote": "Each has exactly one of four standings:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F1.%20Vocabulary%20of%20standing",
          "start_line": 21
        }
      ],
      "key": "foundational_standing",
      "label": "Foundational standing"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Four distinct meanings of join: categorical join is a map between categories; composition boundary joins successive steps; result join combines closed results under one decision rule; relation join is an equality join of participants in an enclosing relation. Unqualified join in a result context means result join.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 50,
          "quote": "Four uses of \"join\" in this corpus are distinct",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F1.%20Vocabulary%20of%20standing",
          "start_line": 42
        }
      ],
      "key": "join_meanings",
      "label": "Distinct join meanings"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Subject is the exact thing a claim, representation or transformation concerns, specified by inventory and bytes or semantic addresses. Representation is bytes, text, model or program standing for it; equal representations do not establish equal meaning. Interpretation is a declared representation-to-meaning contract stating reliable distinctions and residuals within a Context, whose declared scope includes subject, definitions, basis and admissible observations.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 59,
          "quote": "| Interpretation | a declared contract from representation to meaning within a context; says which distinctions a consumer may rely on and which are residual | P1 |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology",
          "start_line": 56
        }
      ],
      "key": "subject_representation_interpretation_context",
      "label": "Subject, representation, interpretation and context"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Outcome U is a selected, owner-defined, directly verifiable state with an acceptance interval against which transformation adequacy is judged. Object O is a subject with scoped identity (authority, kind, value), boundary, current state and declared material relations; identity survives admissible state and encoding change.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 61,
          "quote": "| Outcome U | a selected, owner-defined, directly verifiable state with an acceptance interval; adequacy of a transformation is judged against it | P2 |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology",
          "start_line": 60
        }
      ],
      "key": "outcome_and_object",
      "label": "Outcome U and Object O"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Actor performs a step within a capability envelope of context, attention, time and capability; roles are occupied relations selected by configuration, not actor kinds. Step is a bounded unit comprising intake, selected context, grant, execution and output. A step-produced fact is a judgment, residual, observation or authority decision produced by a step that cannot be reacquired from declared sources; reproducible observations are excluded.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 64,
          "quote": "| Step | one bounded unit of work: intake, selected context, grant, execution, output | P0 |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology",
          "start_line": 62
        }
      ],
      "key": "actor_step_and_produced_fact",
      "label": "Actor, step and step-produced fact"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Claim is an assertion with subject, property, basis, witness relation and falsifier. Evidence is exact admitted observations or artifacts carrying provenance, subject identity, basis and scope; availability in a frame confers neither semantic truth nor decision authority. Judgment is a recorded evaluation over an unchanged subject with evaluator, decision, evidence, scope and revising observation. Residual is material uncertainty, omission, loss or unproven relation remaining until authorised disposition.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 69,
          "quote": "| Claim | an assertion with subject, property, basis, witness relation and falsifier | P6 |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology",
          "start_line": 65
        }
      ],
      "key": "claim_evidence_judgment_residual",
      "label": "Claim, evidence, judgment and residual"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Basis is the exact state binding a step, claim, result or evaluation, including every material version, identity, time, topology, authority, configuration and evidence coordinate. It is immutable or moving with declared advancement detection and reevaluation conditions; mixed bases require an explicit composite relation. Materiality is evaluation-relative: lawful change can change a result, invalidate its basis, change admissible evidence, change who may perform a material operation, or change who may decide an evaluation or material operation; uncertainty and closure are material on the same grounds.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 71,
          "quote": "materiality is evaluation-relative, so a relation material in one frame may be irrelevant in another",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology",
          "start_line": 70
        }
      ],
      "key": "basis_and_materiality",
      "label": "Basis and materiality"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Evaluation is a declared question with known admissible result family, evidence basis, decision authority and operation authority where material action occurs; it may seek judgment, prediction, construction, comparison, proof, refusal, classification or action recommendation. An evaluation family needs the same material-relation manifold, authority basis and result algebra; similar wording is insufficient.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 113,
          "quote": "similar wording does not prove that two evaluations share a family",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FFrame%20objects",
          "start_line": 112
        }
      ],
      "key": "evaluation_and_family",
      "label": "Evaluation and evaluation family"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Workspace is the bounded material reasoned over or acted on, potentially including constitutional sources, assets, designs, implementations, tools, runtime, evidence, history, dependencies and human decisions; a repository is only one possible carrier. Material-relation manifold is the finite jointly interpretable variables, entities, identities, states, relations, invariants, authorities, observations and exclusions an evaluation family needs; charts may overlap and imply neither geometric mathematics nor runtime abstraction. Coordinate is the complete locally required basis, identity, lineage, input, evidence and authority position. Topology is the visible relation structure of entities, capabilities, authorities, lifecycle state spaces, adjacencies and seams at one basis, not necessarily the whole workspace.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 117,
          "quote": "no geometric mathematics or runtime abstraction is implied",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FFrame%20objects",
          "start_line": 114
        }
      ],
      "key": "workspace_manifold_coordinate_topology",
      "label": "Workspace, manifold, coordinate and topology"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Capability envelope specifies competence, context capacity, tools, access, applicable evaluation or assurance independence, and configuration required for activation and evaluation. Reference frame is a finite specification of attention, coordinates, authority, evidence and result relations for one declared evaluation family over a stated basis. Activated frame binds a declaration to exact evaluation, workspace subject, basis, actor and evidence-acquisition boundary; declaration reuse is not proof of activation correctness.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 120,
          "quote": "a reusable declaration is not evidence that a particular activation was correct",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FFrame%20objects",
          "start_line": 118
        }
      ],
      "key": "reference_frame_and_activation",
      "label": "Reference frame, activation and capability envelope"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Frame set is the human-declared available family for an outcome or workspace, potentially with overlaps, alternatives, specialist, exploratory and separately activated assurance frames; it need not be a partition, tree, stack or exhaustive ontology. Active configuration consists of frame activations and declared result relations for one evaluation or decision, whether one frame or multiple disjoint, overlapping or composed frames. Lens is a reusable question family; abstract authority, capability and reuse, temporal behaviour, transport and proof may be lenses. Role allocates responsibility or admitted authority; persona changes approach or salience; abstraction hides or contracts detail; component is a structural subject part. None is a reference frame by identity alone.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 124,
          "quote": "one component may appear in several frames and one frame may cross several components",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FFrame%20objects",
          "start_line": 121
        }
      ],
      "key": "frame_configuration_and_related_categories",
      "label": "Frame set, configuration, lens, role, persona, abstraction and component"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Authority is identity-bearing permission to operate or dispose acceptance within scope. Its distinct kinds are frame-set authority, the human authority declaring or accepting a project frame set; semantic authority, owner of a material relation's meaning; evaluation authority, role permitted to evaluate; operation authority, owner-issued grant to construct, transform, admit, publish or mutate a material subject; and decision authority, role permitted to accept, reject or otherwise dispose a result. Actor identity identifies the human, agent, tool or process and is not authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 139,
          "quote": "These may coincide in a lawful case and\nnever collapse by default.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FAuthority%20kinds",
          "start_line": 128
        },
        {
          "end_line": 68,
          "quote": "an identity-bearing permission to operate or to dispose acceptance within a scope",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology",
          "start_line": 68
        }
      ],
      "key": "authority_kinds",
      "label": "Authority kinds"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An explicitly admitted bounded proxy may exercise human authority where governing identity and authority law permits; its identity remains distinct from human-authority identity and its action stays inside the existing grant.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 176,
          "quote": "A proxy acts only inside its existing grant.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FAuthority%20kinds",
          "start_line": 171
        }
      ],
      "key": "bounded_proxy",
      "label": "Bounded human-authority proxy"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Frame disjointness is structural absence of shared material variables, coordinates, evidence or result dependencies for declared evaluations. Evaluation independence is separate activation without consumption or influence of one another's unclosed working results; shared subjects, overlapping manifolds and later conjunction of closed results are permitted. Assurance independence is claim-relative non-authorship, evidence acquisition, reference exposure, actor and configuration separation required by governing assurance law.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 204,
          "quote": "Three relations stay distinct",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FDisjointness%20and%20independence",
          "start_line": 192
        }
      ],
      "key": "disjointness_and_independence",
      "label": "Structural disjointness, evaluation independence and assurance independence"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Reference frame F is ⟨Q,B,M,C,I,A,E,X,R,J,K,D⟩: evaluation family; workspace and topology basis; material-relation manifold; coordinate/equality law; applicable invariants; authority relation; admissible evidence/provenance law; exclusions/prohibited assumptions; closed result algebra or refinement into it; invalidation/rechart condition; actor capability envelope; optional dependencies, overlaps, inputs and outputs involving other frames. It specifies an evaluation contract's finite attention scope, not a mandatory schema or runtime carrier.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 226,
          "quote": "A reference frame F is the tuple ⟨Q, B, M, C, I, A, E, X, R, J, K, D⟩",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FThe%20frame%20tuple",
          "start_line": 208
        }
      ],
      "key": "frame_tuple",
      "label": "Frame tuple"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The recursive-product-taxonomy bounded context is owned here under the selected complete basis and carries unchanged concept URNs with prefix urn:stdo:concept:recursive-product-taxonomy:. Substrate is a lower product/runtime used to build products. Development product is the role of one exact released-product install as builder for a source project, creating no Product identity and transferring no meaning or authority. Source project is the mutable workspace building the next cut. Release cut is an immutable annotated release-candidate boundary over a published feature set. Product is the released immutable thing resulting from acceptance of that cut. Install is a stamped workspace instance of that Product. Artifact is published output built by a source project or configured install. Product definition is the present-tense source-project authority surface, conventionally PRODUCT.md, defining current WHAT for decomposition and distinct from the released artifact.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 249,
          "quote": "Each role is an\nobject with its own identity",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FProduct%20lineage%20objects",
          "start_line": 233
        }
      ],
      "key": "product_lineage_objects",
      "label": "Recursive Product lineage objects"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Distinct release neighbours: candidate source is mutable source-project state constructing a possible successor and non-operative for released consumers; release subject is the exact declared member set or artifact bound by one cut and described by its release-scoped claims; version-line selector is a mutable alias for the highest-ordinal published cut on one project's version line, useful for discovery but neither immutable identity nor sufficient exact basis. Co-location in a carrier does not imply release-subject membership.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 280,
          "quote": "A selector or a\nmutable carrier may move; a release cut never moves.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/FOUNDATIONS.md#FOUNDATIONS%2F2.%20Ontology%2FProduct%20lineage%20objects",
          "start_line": 265
        }
      ],
      "key": "release_neighbour_objects",
      "label": "Candidate source, release subject and version-line selector"
    },
    {
